---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";

const results = [
  { value: "Bottom → top", label: "company-wide DC standings" },
  { value: "+54.5%", label: "standings improvement" },
  { value: "−15%", label: "overtime expense" },
  { value: "+7.3%", label: "delivery-window success" },
];
const stack = [
  "Team leadership",
  "Culture building",
  "Coaching & training",
  "KPI design",
  "Power BI",
  "Excel",
  "Manifest & route analysis",
  "DOT compliance",
];
const title = "Distribution Center Turnaround — Bottom Quartile to Top";
const desc =
  "Taking a multi-million-dollar distribution center from the bottom quartile of the company standings to the top — by building a positive team culture first, then coaching 30 people to use their data tools every day.";
---

<CaseStudy
  title={title}
  desc={desc}
  label="Case Study · Operations & Leadership"
  heading="DC Turnaround"
  results={results}
  stack={stack}
  published="2026-07-26"
  modified="2026-10-09"
>
  <section class="block">
    <h2>The situation</h2>
    <p>
      I took over a multi-million-dollar distribution center sitting in the bottom
      quartile of its company-wide performance standings. Overtime was high, inventory
      variance was unexplained, and delivery windows were missed often enough that
      customers noticed. Nobody could see the problem in time to act on it —
      performance was something you learned about after the month closed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>What I did</h2>
    <h3>Built the team first</h3>
    <p>
      Thirty people — hiring, mentoring, and training, and above all a positive,
      accountable culture where problems get raised on shift instead of discovered
      at month-end. Overtime doesn't drop 15% because a report says it should. It
      drops because supervisors trust the numbers, the crew trusts the supervisors,
      and people speak up early.
    </p>
    <h3>Made the tools the team's own</h3>
    <p>
      The building already had Power BI and Excel; they just weren't part of how the
      team worked. I coached supervisors to use Power BI for daily order monitoring,
      so disruptions surfaced the morning they happened, and to plan each shift in
      Excel the same way with the same inputs. Streamlined workflows and that shared
      planning lifted warehouse efficiency roughly 10%. The metrics that mattered —
      fill, on-time windows, labor hours against volume — became something the team
      looked at together and steered by.
    </p>
    <h3>Attacked the cost line</h3>
    <p>
      Targeted scheduling and process changes cut overtime expense 15% while
      inventory variance improved — the two numbers that usually move in opposite
      directions when a DC is under pressure.
    </p>
    <h3>Fixed delivery reliability</h3>
    <p>
      Driver training, manifest analysis, and tighter DOT compliance raised
      route-delivery window success 7.3%. Analysis pointed at which routes and
      which handoffs were failing; the training closed them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="note">
    Figures are the relative performance improvements reported on my résumé. No
    employer-internal data, reporting, or systems are reproduced here.
  </p>
</CaseStudy>
